name: choices and loops
intro: A program that cannot choose or repeat is a calculator. C gives you
intro: `if`, loops and `switch`, and all of them test a plain number: zero is
intro: false, anything else is true.
intro:
intro:     if (n % 2 == 0) {
intro:         printf("even\n");
intro:     } else if (n > 100) {
intro:         printf("big and odd\n");
intro:     } else {
intro:         printf("odd\n");
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Always use braces on a branch, or at least keep it to one line. A
intro: second line added under a braceless `if` is not part of it.
intro:
intro: Loops: `for` for counting, `while` for "until something happens".
intro: Declare the counter inside the for, so it dies with the loop:
intro:
intro:     for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++)
intro:         printf("%d\n", i);
intro:
intro: `i < 5` runs 0 to 4: five turns. Off-by-one is the classic loop bug, so
intro: say the first and last value out loud before you write the test.
intro: `do { ... } while (cond);` runs the body once before it tests; you will
intro: need it rarely.
intro:
intro: `switch` picks by exact integer value. Each case runs on into the next
intro: one until a `break`, so forget one and you get both:
intro:
intro:     switch (n) {
intro:     case 0:
intro:         printf("zero\n");
intro:         break;
intro:     case 1:
intro:     case 2:
intro:         printf("one or two\n");
intro:         break;
intro:     default:
intro:         printf("something else\n");
intro:         break;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Stacked labels like `case 1:` `case 2:` share one body on purpose. Keep
intro: a `default`: it is where the values you did not think of land.
intro:
intro: Everything here is fixed loops -- no input yet.
---
q: fizz.c -- fizz and buzz
spec: Print the numbers 1 to 15, one per line, except: multiples of 3 print
spec: `fizz`, multiples of 5 print `buzz`, multiples of both print `fizzbuzz`.
file: fizz.c
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	for (int i = 1; i <= 15; i++) {
prog: 		if (i % 15 == 0) {
prog: 			printf("fizzbuzz\n");
prog: 		} else if (i % 3 == 0) {
prog: 			printf("fizz\n");
prog: 		} else if (i % 5 == 0) {
prog: 			printf("buzz\n");
prog: 		} else {
prog: 			printf("%d\n", i);
prog: 		}
prog: 	}
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: fizz
case: fizz | tail -n 1
case: fizz | sed -n 9,10p
hint: test the multiple of both first, or 15 stops at fizz
